So, for example, if any of these values are empty, parsing would fail.

Wait, in the test data for verifyPositions, the line "*GS06,359913060650380,152248050718,,SYS:G3C;V1.38;V05,GPS:A;10;N31.914370;E35.914640;0;0,COT:689,ADC:0.18;3.55,DTT:4025;E6;0;0;0;1#" has in DTT: "E6;0;0;0;1" – wait, DTT is a separate fragment.

Wait, perhaps there's a GSM fragment where values like registration or signal strength are empty.

Looking through the test cases, for example:

One test has "GSM:0;0;310;260;C76D;9F1D;-85" — that would split into "0", "0", "310", etc., but another case has "GSM:;;310;260;C76D;9F1D;-85" — the values would have "", "", etc.

So when doing Integer.parseInt(values[index++]), if it's an empty string, that's going to throw a NumberFormatException.

Wait, but wait, in the code for "GSM", the code does:

index +=1; // registration status
index +=1; // signal strength

But it doesn't check if values at index are empty before parsing. So if any of the values is empty, parsing to int would fail.

So if GSM fragment is empty in any of these spots, it would crash.

Similarly, the ADC case:

In ADC, after setting KEY_POWER, it then checks if index < values.length to set BATTERY, etc. But if the next value is empty, trying to parse it as double will throw.

Looking through all the cases, it seems that the error occurs when the code is trying to parse an empty string into a number.

So to fix this, I think I need to add checks before parsing.

